;; The DEFAULT tier: the three knobs a session starts from. Anything left out
;; falls through -- to the provider's own default model, or to no reasoning
;; effort at all -- so a config naming only a provider is the simplest case.
;;
;;   :provider          a provider name from providers.edn or the built-in table
;;   :model             a model id THAT provider declares (optional; leaving it
;;                      out uses the provider's default)
;;   :reasoning-effort  forwarded to providers that understand it
;;
;; THREE KNOBS, and only three: a model's context window and its maximum output
;; tokens are NOT here. Those describe what a model IS, so they live on that
;; model's entry in providers.edn, and writing one here is a named failure rather
;; than a value somebody quietly drops.
;;
;; Everything else about a provider -- its endpoint, its protocol, what each of
;; its models accepts and how much they hold -- comes from the catalog, not from
;; here. To add a model, edit providers.edn (or use one the built-in table already
;; knows); to change an endpoint, edit the provider's entry. Naming an endpoint
;; here is only meaningful in the INLINE form below, which describes a provider
;; instead of naming one.

{:provider :openrouter :model "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.5" :reasoning-effort "high"}

;; THE INLINE FORM (the escape hatch): describe a provider instead of naming one,
;; for trying an endpoint without registering it. Requires :protocol, :base-url
;; and :model; :input/:output are optional and declaring nothing means the entry
;; promises nothing (nothing then checks its input). The two counts are optional
;; here too, and are REPORTED rather than enforced -- see providers.edn.example:
;; :max-output-tokens is not sent to the vendor and :context-window blocks no run.
;;
;;   {:protocol :openai-completions
;;    :base-url "https://some-endpoint/v1"
;;    :model    "some-model"
;;    :input    #{:text :image}
;;    :output   #{:text}
;;    :context-window 128000
;;    :max-output-tokens 4096}
